[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Oracle Database Recovery Scenarios: Complete and Point-in-Time Recovery"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Oracle database recovery is the process of restoring a database to a consistent and usable state after a failure. Two common recovery scenarios are Complete Recovery and Point-in-Time Recovery (PITR). This tutorial will cover both scenarios, outlining their purposes, procedures, and considerations."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Complete Recovery"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Complete recovery restores the database to the state it was in immediately before the failure. This is the ideal recovery scenario, as no data is lost. Complete recovery requires a valid backup and all archived redo logs generated since that backup."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"1.1 Prerequisites for Complete Recovery"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A valid database backup (full or incremental)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"All archived redo logs generated since the backup was taken."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Control file(s) must be intact or restorable."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"1.2 Steps for Complete Recovery"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"# 1. Restore the database from the backup:
rman target / 
restore database; 
recover database; 
alter database open resetlogs;"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation of the commands:
- 'rman target /' connects to the target database using Recovery Manager (RMAN).
- 'restore database;' restores the database files from the backup.
- 'recover database;' applies the archived redo logs to bring the database to a consistent state.
- 'alter database open resetlogs;' opens the database and resets the online redo logs. This step is crucial after recovery."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"1.3 Considerations for Complete Recovery"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure sufficient disk space is available to restore the database and apply the redo logs."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Verify the integrity of the backup and archived redo logs before starting the recovery process."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The 'resetlogs' option is required after recovery. This creates a new incarnation of the database."},{"order":16,"type":"heading","text":"2. Point-in-Time Recovery (PITR)"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Point-in-Time Recovery (PITR) restores the database to a specific point in time in the past. This is useful when data corruption or logical errors occur, and you need to revert the database to a state before the error occurred. PITR also requires a valid backup and archived redo logs."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"2.1 Prerequisites for Point-in-Time Recovery"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A valid database backup (full or incremental)."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Archived redo logs up to the desired point in time."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The SCN (System Change Number) or timestamp of the desired point in time."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"2.2 Steps for Point-in-Time Recovery"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"# 1. Restore the database from the backup:
rman target / 
run {
  set until time "TO_DATE('YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS', 'YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS')";
  restore database;
  recover database;
  alter database open resetlogs;
}"},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"# Alternatively, using SCN:
rman target / 
run {
  set until scn <scn_value>;
  restore database;
  recover database;
  alter database open resetlogs;
}"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation of the commands:
- 'set until time "TO_DATE('YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS', 'YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS')";' specifies the target point in time for the recovery. Replace 'YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS' with the desired date and time.
- 'set until scn <scn_value>;' specifies the target SCN for the recovery. Replace '<scn_value>' with the desired SCN.
- The remaining commands ('restore database;', 'recover database;', 'alter database open resetlogs;') are the same as in complete recovery."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"2.3 Considerations for Point-in-Time Recovery"},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"PITR can result in data loss, as changes made after the specified point in time will be lost."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Carefully choose the point in time or SCN to minimize data loss while correcting the issue."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider using a duplicate database for testing the PITR process before applying it to the production database."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"After PITR, any objects created after the recovery point will be lost. You may need to re-create them."},{"order":31,"type":"heading","text":"3. Conclusion"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding Complete Recovery and Point-in-Time Recovery is crucial for maintaining the integrity and availability of your Oracle database. Choose the appropriate recovery scenario based on the nature of the failure and the desired outcome. Always test your recovery procedures in a non-production environment before applying them to production."}]
